orts §2, the risk-utility test asks whether a design creates unreasonable risk. No proof of actual harm is required. Courts have evaluated design defects in medical devices and automotive software — systems whose internal complexity exceeds lay understanding. The Federal Food, Drug, and Cosmetic Act requires pharmaceutical manufacturers to demonstrate safety before deployment, not after harm surfaces. AI opacity forecloses even expert inspection. If no engineer, regulator, or court can examine the actual decision-making process, the system cannot meet any accountability standard society has constructed for engineered systems at societal scale. Developers benefit from deployment. The public bears the cost of uninspectable decisions in lending, hiring, and medical diagnosis. The enforcement mechanism is tort litigation under existing product liability doctrine. Pre-deployment certification requirements modeled on FDA drug approval would supplement this. The Biden administration's October 2023 Executive Order on AI already directs agencies to establish safety testing standards. A political coalition for pre-deployment oversight is forming. Industry opposition remains substantial.\n\nThe strongest opposing case holds that interpretability research is advancing rapidly. Many beneficial systems operate without full transparency. A prima facie standard would block deployment of systems delivering real value in healthcare, education, and scientific research. The outcomes are real — and that is exactly why governance is needed to ensure they continue. The FDA's pre-market approval process has not blocked pharmaceutical innovation. It has made pharmaceutical deployment survivable. A bridge that cannot bear its load today is defective today, regardless of whether materials science may eventually produce stronger steel. The legal standard assesses defect at time of deployment, not at some future state of the art. Two assumptions bear disclosure. First, this argument depends on \"sufficient transparency\" being achievable for systems whose decisions affect human welfare — an empirical question current evidence does not resolve in the deployer's favor. Second, the extension of products liability from physical products to algorithmic systems rests on medical device and automotive software precedent. Direct AI-specific case law remains sparse.\n\nEvery engineered system operating at societal scale must satisfy inspectability requirements before deployment. Aviation, pharmaceuticals, medical devices, financial infrastructure, nuclear power — each was subjected to inspection regimes after its own crisis revealed the cost of opacity. The 1962 Kefauver-Harris Amendment required drug manufacturers to prove efficacy and safety before market entry because thalidomide had demonstrated what uninspectable deployment costs. We spent centuries building the institutional guardrails that make deployment of powerful technologies survivable. On what authority does any developer claim the right to remove them? The question is not whether AI is too important to regulate. The question is whether any technology can be exempt from the guardrails that govern every other — and what happens to the accountability mechanisms when one is.",
