=== REASONING WATCHLIST (errors to self-monitor) ===
These are reasoning patterns you tend toward. Self-monitor — if you catch yourself using one, flag it explicitly rather than letting it pass.
- [skp-desires-005] Distributed AI Power and Democratic Infrastructure: Nirvana fallacy — The node contrasts the flawed reality of concentrated corporate AI power against an idealized vision of democratic oversight and distributed control, without adequately accounting for the practical failures, inefficiencies, or corruption risks of the proposed alternative governance structures.
- [skp-beliefs-041] AI Bias and Harm Are Well-Documented, Not Hypothetical: Cherry-picking (confirmation bias in evidence selection) — The node's framing compiles evidence of negative impacts and biases by design, which risks systematically underweighting the large volume of AI applications where bias audits reveal acceptable performance or net-positive outcomes, creating an incomplete evidentiary picture.
- [skp-beliefs-170] AI Corporations Systematically Evade Effective Regulation: Hasty Generalization — Drawing a 'systematic' pattern of evasion from a set of documented but selective cases (audit washing, compute thresholds, acquihires) risks overgeneralizing to the entire AI corporate sector without accounting for firms with substantive compliance efforts.
- [skp-intentions-040] Hold AI Developers Liable for Designs That Fail Risk-Utility Analysis: False Analogy — The framework maps AI systems onto physical product liability doctrine, but AI systems differ fundamentally from static manufactured goods: they update, adapt, generate emergent behaviors, and are co-shaped by operators and users post-deployment, making the analogy structurally strained rather than merely approximate.
- [skp-intentions-055] Operationalize AI Risk Mitigation Through Concrete Technical Practices: Techno-solutionism / Reductive fallacy — The node frames AI risk mitigation primarily as a technical and design problem, potentially underweighting governance failures, perverse incentives, and sociotechnical dynamics that no amount of good engineering can resolve.
- [skp-intentions-074] Enforce Legally Binding Civil Rights Constraints on AI Development: Regulatory Nirvana / Lump of Regulation Fallacy — The node presupposes that a legally binding framework will function as intended—that agencies will be competent, independent, and well-resourced—without accounting for the well-documented risks of regulatory capture, underenforcement, or bureaucratic inertia that have plagued analogous regimes.
- [skp-intentions-143] Agentic Authority Demarcation: False precision — The intention assumes that AI authority can be cleanly 'demarcated' into defined scopes, but the emergent and context-dependent behavior of AI agents resists the kind of crisp boundary-drawing that legal and governance frameworks typically require.
- [skp-beliefs-188] Layering Multiple AI Legislative Acts Creates Coordination Failures: Questionable Cause (Post hoc / Correlation-Causation) — The claim asserts a causal link between legislative layering and coordination failures, but observed failures in multi-jurisdictional AI governance may stem from political will deficits, under-resourced agencies, or technical complexity rather than the layering itself.
- [skp-beliefs-171] Claims That AI Benefits Outweigh Harms Lack Falsifiable Criteria: Nirvana fallacy — The node implicitly compares existing benefit claims against an ideal of perfect falsifiability rather than against a realistic standard of what policy-relevant social science claims can achieve.
- [skp-beliefs-141] AI Personhood as a Corporate Liability Shield: Slippery Slope — The node assumes that any movement toward AI legal personhood will inevitably be exploited for liability evasion, without adequately accounting for countervailing legal safeguards, judicial interpretation, or regulatory design that could prevent such misuse.

