e liability for deployment-context decisions. The design defect framework's attempt to pin everything on model architecture misallocates accountability away from the actors who control the risk. The political coalition supporting downstream allocation spans open-source developers, deployment platforms, and enterprise users who would face disproportionate liability under an upstream-only framework. The coalition opposing it is frontier labs and regulators who benefit from concentrating compliance burdens at the model level.\n\nA prima facie standard requiring mechanistic explainability as a precondition for deployment would foreclose systems already delivering measurable medical benefits. The Skeptic observed that if every deployed system fails the standard, the standard describes the problem rather than solving it. Push that to its moral conclusion. Google's diabetic retinopathy detection system achieved specialist-level accuracy in a JAMA-published study in 2016 and was deployed in Thailand's clinical screening program — without full mechanistic interpretability. Systems like this one are already catching disease earlier than human screening alone. A prima facie defect standard would block them. The opportunity cost is not abstract. It is quantifiable in patients who would have been diagnosed earlier and were not, because the system was never deployed. A standard no one can meet is not a safety framework. It is a prohibition. And prohibitions on AI deployment carry a body count among the people abundance would have reached. The question is not whether opacity is a problem. It is whether a legal framework that cannot distinguish between a known defect and a frontier limitation is the right tool to solve it.",
